eorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b63f5657-355a-5f0a-a214-2065b7a76d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1061 20109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "babad562-3d88-57e5-a6c7-a8b53bec4956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1061 20111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46208d38-6fce-5121-8f5a-512e318a7731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1061 20113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4880d3b1-8971-5b5f-8646-d786ae6c89fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1061 31946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e492707b-f646-51d5-b4f4-34c984760081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1064 "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$* \n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1065 3569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e561ab8-38f8-5ec1-a285-6559f35aa7cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450747 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450748 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450749 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47d1848e-d69a-5675-a8fe-fca5e30be264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450750 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450751 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450752 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A-7I$ is singular\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f77ae0b2-0cbc-5c99-a8dc-82a79dae8fdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272161 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453072 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453073 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453074 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(B-4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(B-4I)=4$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6fdd6535-d1b6-5f9c-9675-a9cfd3d10476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362249 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(B-4I)\\ne 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453078 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453079 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453080 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C-I$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(C-I)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1c970e5-75bc-5ce7-946f-a4ae8b32b111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362252 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453084 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453085 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453086 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "651bcec7-c2ce-52d1-b1e2-d8f7e6384008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362254 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921864081